[#4471](/kb/incidents/4471) says *known, one issue, one fix queued*. Its reports show more
than one problem (the table counts them). The parser fix that is queued closes one of them; each
problem has its own ticket now.

<Callout kind="warn">
Not one issue: one problem, one fix, one ticket each. Don't close a report from #4471 against the parser
fix until you have checked which problem it shows.
</Callout>

## The split

<SplitTable ticket={4471} {tickets} />

The counts are built from `reports-4471.json` and `desk-notes.json` at render time: one id, one
report; the tenant's locale and booking form decide `parser` or `hint` (`#lib/kb/causes`); the desk's
`oneDayMove` list decides `one-day`. If the desk merges or re-reads reports, this table follows.

## The three problems

**`parser` — day and month swapped.** The shared parser reads every date month first. Our tenants
write the day first, so 7 April comes out as 4 July. Every channel, every day-first tenant.
*"Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July."* This is the bug #4471 describes, and the fix that is queued.

**`hint` — the booking-v3 form tells people to type month first.** Tenants on `booking-v3` show
`MM/DD/YYYY` under the date field. Their people type what the hint says; the form reads it the
tenant's way, day first. It reads the same as `parser` in a report, but it only comes through the
form, only for booking-v3 tenants, and the parser fix does not touch it: the form hint has to
change.

**`one-day` — the day moves by one, the month stays.** *"Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday."*
*"Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday. Same meeting."* No day/month swap
can do that. It points at time zones (an invite rendered in the viewer's zone, a date stored as
midnight UTC). The desk already read these reports this way (`oneDayMove`) and still filed them
under the parser.

## Why it was merged

Subjects. [Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges on the subject line, and
the three problems share the same subjects: *"Date is wrong"*, *"Wrong day on the invite"*, even
*"Month and day swapped?"* come in for each of them. The subject says the date is wrong; the body
and the tenant say how. Intake's tag is a guess from the reporter's browser and
disagrees with the tenant on dozens of these reports, so it can't split them either.

## For the desk

- Triage a date report by **tenant and channel first**, then the body: day/month swapped through
  a booking-v3 form is `hint`; one day off is `one-day`; any other swap is `parser`.
- Decision 0001 stays as it is (decisions are not edited). Splitting on subject alone hid problems
  since April; a new record should replace it.

